WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.349 --> 00:00:02.849
[music]

2
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.120
[Instructor] All right. So let's
get started with Prototype 3.

3
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.580
Now I'm going to import
the Project files,

4
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.604
and set up my new Unity Project.

5
00:00:12.604 --> 00:00:15.351
But since we've already done
that let me just open up Unity

6
00:00:15.351 --> 00:00:17.808
so that we can get going.
Now I'm going to go

7
00:00:17.808 --> 00:00:19.621
into the scenes Folder,

8
00:00:19.621 --> 00:00:21.620
underneath my Project window.

9
00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.299
Open up "Prototype 3",
by double-clicking on it.

10
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.200
I'm going to delete my sample scene
by right-clicking on it.

11
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.090
Delete. Let me reposition
my Camera here.

12
00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.343
I'm on the other side, okay. Cool.

13
00:00:38.343 --> 00:00:40.340
So that looks like what's
in our Game View,

14
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.255
and then last but not least,
once I've had all my Assets imported

15
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.020
you can actually select
the Background Object

16
00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.430
in the scene on the right side,

17
00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.230
you'll see in the Inspector
for the Background,

18
00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.650
this "Sprite Renderer".

19
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.620
Now a Sprite Renderer is a Component
that enables you to render

20
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.070
or to see images in your game,
and in your environment.

21
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.217
So in this case,
we have this Background image.

22
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.152
So we use our Sprite Renderer
to actually see that image in our scene,

23
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.382
especially when you're making 2D games,

24
00:01:15.382 --> 00:01:17.735
Sprite Renderers are
particularly useful.

25
00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:22.011
Or if you have a character with items
or they're carrying something.

26
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.178
So with our Sprite Renderer,
on the right

27
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.410
there's an little property called
Sprite, and you can see currently

28
00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.790
it's set to "Background_Town"
all the way

29
00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.964
on the right side of the Inspector.

30
00:01:35.414 --> 00:01:38.320
There's this little circle icon,
it's a radio button

31
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.140
so you can actually click on it.

32
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.143
And there are all these different
options.

33
00:01:42.753 --> 00:01:45.550
So there's in fact actually 3 different
Backgrounds we can use.

34
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:47.618
So you can select one
that works for you best.

35
00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.770
I kind of liked this forest scene.
It's very natural.

36
00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.570
It's got a lot of bright colors
on it. So now I can just close out

37
00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.572
of this window, and we have
a Background selected.

38
00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.363
All right. So what you're going
to do is open up the Unity Hub,

39
00:02:05.363 --> 00:02:07.850
and create a new
Prototype through Project.

40
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:12.192
Use the link below in the steps
to download the Project starter files.

41
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.800
Make sure to open
the Prototype 3 scene

42
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.800
and delete the sample scene.

43
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.120
And then you can select
the Background Object in the Hierarchy.

44
00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:23.730
And then in the Sprite Renderer
Component, you can go to Sprite

45
00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.371
and select a new Background image
to use if you want to use the city,

46
00:02:27.371 --> 00:02:30.472
nature or town Background.
So now it's your turn.

47
00:02:31.014 --> 00:02:33.414
[music]
